ng to serve your patients well  when we come to doing qc on sars  kobe 2 methods we face the same  options that we would with any other  method  we could use a manufacturer or package  insert ranges  we could use full west guard rules  or we could do something in the middle  now the use of package insert ranges  on there's a long history of of knowing  those ranges are too big  they're probably too wide they'll be  insensitive  they won't pick up errors but they do  serve a purpose  and that is when you're just starting  out  if you have no other information about  the performance of the method  you use that package insert information  first  and then once you get 20 data points  once you get a month of information  on performance you quickly switch to  that  another tradition in a lot of testing is  throw all the westgard rules at a test  and some some labs will throw westgard  rules at everything  that might have been good in 1981 when  the westgard rules were  first introduced but today  um if we have selected a method with  good performance  full westgard rules may in fact be too  strict  it may cause us to define  some things as out of control or reject  runs  when actually the problems we're  detecting have no clinical  importance  what we would like to do is find out a  way  to choose just enough westgard rules  as the method requires if we don't know  the performance of the method  however if we have no characterization  we don't know if it's  a high quality method or a low quality  method  in the absence of that performance  understanding  using full westgard rules may be of  maybe a useful resort  if you don't know anything yes go ahead  and use the westgard rules but hopefully  after some weeks of operating the method  you will gain a better understanding and  be able to customize and tailor your use  of qc rules  and what we'd like is that this middle  ground of  the use of westgard rules we  these days on on methods outside of sars  kobe 2 testing  ther